 Just a moment, permit me, dear colleague.

56
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:24,290
 This apparatus appears interesting, don't you think?

57
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:32,570
 ...swinging precisely at my pulse rate. Let's check yours.

58
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:41,790
 Your pulse is more rapid, old friend. You have humors that are galloping away.

59
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:46,750
 Ah, you only need to look. It will slow down with time. It depends on the swing. It's a useless toy.

60
00:04:46,750 --> 00:04:57,530
 No, sir, not at all. You see, it's not the length of the swing. The length of the pendulum determines how fast it swings.

61
00:04:57,530 --> 00:05:07,710
 Much too rapid. It's a bad sign. A little bloodletting would do you some good.

62
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:13,890
 Young man, your invention will be very useful to us.

63
00:05:13,890 --> 00:05:16,950
 You are quite right. You can measure time with it.

64
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:21,310
 Galileo went on to use his invention in making clocks.

65
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:30,610
 This is the only one in a long line of discoveries that he made.

66
00:05:30,610 --> 00:05:59,820
 Farther it sinks down.

67
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:06,530
 What is dense sinks quicker.

68
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:11,100
 In this way, we can test whether a liquid is more dense than water.

69
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:17,790
 At the age of 25, Galileo is appointed professor of mathematics.

70
00:06:17,790 --> 00:06:30,560
 You remember Galileo? We were kids.

71
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:32,540
 We used to joke about jumping.

72
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:35,620
 This time you'll see you're wrong as can be.

73
